A||||5||AJ88||Newbie q's||||1||
Z||000000||AJ88||11-20-2001||12:14 AM||||Hey all, I'm tossing the idea around in my head about doing a Fiero project as my first engine swap.<P>I know this question probably gets asked a zillion times, but I can't find a specific answer in the archives.<P>If I go ahead with this project, I'll get a V6 Fiero, and swap in a 3.4L.  I'm wondering which 3.4L *short blocks* would work.  I need this to look as stock as possible to avoid the Smog Police.  3.4L Camaros are very rare around here, and I figure I might as well do a port + polish on the heads while the car is apart.<P>Obviously I need a pushrod 3.4L, not the DOHC version.  Is there a list of cars that were equipped with these engines?<P>I found a really cool site, but it only lists new engines:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/other/34_main.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||MooCow||11-20-2001||01:00 AM||||Hey welcome to the forum.  Any question you have on the 3.4 swap just talk to Ed Parks he is the man.  I got mine done from him and it is great work.  His web site also has info on the swap: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <BR>Also you can email him at: fierofactory@juno.com.<BR>Hope that helps you out.  Also I think 93-95 Firebirds you can use too.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forum_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||tgowens||11-20-2001||01:07 AM||||The 3.4L engine you need is the pushrod, rear wheel drive version for the '93 - '95 Camaro and Fierbird. There were other 3.4's for Grand Prix and Bonneville but they are the front wheel drive version and will be far more complicated to swap. The 3.4L pusrod is the easiest in my opinon (we do several here at the shop every week) -- you will need to drill for the starter on the opposite side from where it was originally mounted, weld a stud on the front lower shock mount for the a/c bracket as well as a few other fairly simple modifications.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||AJ88||11-20-2001||01:17 AM||||What makes the other 3.4's more complicated to swap?  I haven't done too much research into it, but it seems like all 3.4 short blocks would be the same with the exception of the starter holes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||11-20-2001||05:00 AM||||The other 3.4L engines are not the pushrod engines - they are the overhead cam engines - there is quite a bit of difference in the two - including the wiring harness and computer -- the pushrod engine is the easiest.... I'd do a 4.9L Cadillac before I would do a 3.4 DOHC......||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||11-20-2001||07:01 AM||||There are 3.4s (actually 3400) in the front wheel drive cars that are not DOHC, but they are still *not* a good candidate for a swap. They have redesigned heads, made of aluminum. None of the Fiero stuff will bolt on. You can't use the Fiero heads on this shortblock, either, without changing pistons. Otherwise, you'll end up with 7:1 compression. <BR>Check this link to Ed Parks' site... <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
